/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md | sed -n '55,175p'; nl -ba docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md | sed -n '176,330p'; nl -ba docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md | sed -n '331,510p'" in /Users/user/code-projects/chief-command
 succeeded in 0ms:
    55	2. **Prompt injection** — poisoned text in a file, dependency, issue, or web page turns a
    56	   fooled-but-authorized agent into an attacker's hands. The load-bearing threat, because
    57	   the agent is *allowed* to read the repo.
    58	3. **Mishearing** — road noise, a passenger, a bad transcript.
    59	4. **Malicious code that passes honest review** (round 4) — a payload walked through the
    60	   front door of gates that are working perfectly, gaining the deployed application's own
    61	   production authority (round 5).
    62	
    63	---
    64	
    65	## The trusted computing base — named honestly
    66	
    67	The things that must be trusted: the release-path services (below), the harness, its web
    68	UI, model-response handling, the dispatch channel, evidence capture, OpenClaw
    69	integration, and the OS accounts they run under. The design's job is to make this base
    70	small and *separated* — not to pretend it is one tiny component. Three separations do the
    71	heavy lifting:
    72	
    73	1. **Agents are outside the trusted base entirely** — separate VM, separate identity, no
    74	   keys.
    75	2. **The record-writer is separated from the harness** — release facts are written only
    76	   by the release-path services; the harness submits authenticated observations through a
    77	   narrow interface.
    78	3. **The dangerous powers are separated from each other** (see "Separated powers") — so
    79	   one compromise cannot take merge, signing, deploy, spend, and production data at once.
    80	
    81	---
    82	
    83	## The two human roles — OWNER DECISION A, revised after round 5
    84	
    85	Round 4's decision A said high-authority changes wait for Neill's written sign-off. Sol's
    86	round-5 correction is accepted: **Neill cannot read code, so his approval is a business
    87	authorization, not a technical inspection.** A fooled reviewer writes a fooling
    88	explanation. The two roles are therefore distinct:
    89	
    90	- **The qualified technical reviewer** — an independent human who can actually read the
    91	  change — inspects every high-authority change: the exact patch, its dependency and
    92	  build effects, against the exact package digest that will ship. Their approval is
    93	  recorded against that digest.
    94	- **The owner** separately authorizes the business decision ("yes, I want this change"),
    95	  in plain English, through an authenticated written approval (below). His approval never
    96	  substitutes for the technical inspection.
    97	
    98	**DECIDED (Neill, 2026-07-16): the technical reviewer seat is currently EMPTY, and the
    99	system fails closed.** High-authority changes queue as pending — visible, explained in
   100	plain English, never released — until a qualified reviewer exists (a contractor, a
   101	colleague, a future hire). Naming one later fills the seat without redesign. Everything
   102	outside the high-authority classes still ships autonomously through the model gauntlet.
   103	
   104	### Authenticated approval — proving it was actually Neill
   105	
   106	A tap on a web page proves someone tapped. Owner approval is:
   107	
   108	- bound to the owner's device using a **passkey or biometric confirmation** (device-bound
   109	  hardware credential, not a session cookie),
   110	- **single-use, short-lived, and tied to the exact package digest and the exact action**
   111	  (repo, version, environment),
   112	- consumed atomically before the action, void if the package, `main`, or the request
   113	  changes,
   114	- and never grantable by voice (settled rule).
   115	
   116	Tailscale is network privacy, not user authentication; the web app additionally
   117	authenticates the person, not just the device's network.
   118	
   119	### Exact delivery of anything that matters
   120	
   121	Critical warnings, amounts, consequences, confirmations, and failure reports are played
   122	or displayed **from fixed stored text — never passed through a model that could
   123	paraphrase them.** Telling the voice model "don't paraphrase" is a wish; fixed playback
   124	is the mechanism.
   125	
   126	---
   127	
   128	## Danger classification — by AUTHORITY, not by filenames
   129	
   130	Round 5's second crux. A list of "dangerous files" is trivially evaded (payloads in build
   131	scripts, lockfile-only dependency redirects, transitive dependency swaps, checked-in
   132	generated or minified code, templates that become code at build time, config that becomes
   133	behavior at runtime, dormant loaders activated by later "data" changes, authority split
   134	across routine-looking submissions, cumulative drift). So classification starts from
   135	**the production authority of the thing being changed**:
   136	
   137	- **High-authority by default:** any change to a component that, in production, can read
   138	  private data, write important records, use secrets, move money, administer users, or
   139	  make broad outgoing connections. What the component *can do when running* decides — not
   140	  which file the diff touched.
   141	- **Autonomous release is permitted only for components whose enumerated production
   142	  authority is already so narrow that malicious code inside them cannot cause serious
   143	  harm** (see "Production containment").
   144	- **Fail-closed intake:** unknown file types, binaries, compressed/minified/generated
   145	  output, dependency changes of any kind (including lockfile-only and transitive), build
   146	  and packaging behavior, configuration that is interpreted at runtime (routing rules,
   147	  prompts, feature flags, scheduled-task definitions, templates, policy files, database
   148	  statements), links, sub-project pointers, and large-file pointers **all classify into
   149	  human review.** Anything the classifier cannot positively identify as harmless is
   150	  high-authority.
   151	- **Cumulative, not per-diff:** classification considers everything changed **since the
   152	  last qualified human review** of that component, so five small routine releases cannot
   153	  quietly assemble high authority.
   154	- **The classifier itself is high-authority:** changes to it always require independent
   155	  technical review.
   156	- The classifier is deterministic (derived from the change and the component's enumerated
   157	  authority — never a model's self-report), and deterministic mistakes are still
   158	  mistakes: the enumeration of component authority is reviewed by the technical reviewer
   159	  when the seat is filled, and until then components without an enumeration are treated
   160	  as high-authority.
   161	
   162	**Stated plainly, as Sol required: this reduces how much ships autonomously. That is the
   163	unavoidable price of the round-4 finding, and the owner has accepted it.**
   164	
   165	---
   166	
   167	## Where agents run — the VM, complete profile
   168	
   169	**DECIDED (Neill, 2026-07-15): a dedicated VM on this Mac** — with the honest caveat Sol
   170	required: this protects against *ordinary containment failures*; it does not make hostile
   171	execution on the owner's business Mac risk-free. The VM and the business share one
   172	physical failure domain; a separate machine is materially safer, and the design stays
   173	hardware-agnostic so that migration is a config change. The owner accepts the residual
   174	risk knowingly.
   175	
   176	The profile — all of it, not a subset:
   177	
   178	- **No sharing at all**: no shared folders (not just "not the home folder" — none), no
   179	  clipboard/drag-and-drop, no USB/camera/microphone/Bluetooth passthrough, no
   180	  host-management channel, no credential connections (SSH agent, git credential helper,
   181	  cloud CLI config).
   182	- **No Tailscale identity inside the VM.** No inbound connections to the VM at all.
   183	- **Outgoing traffic only through the named brokers** (below). No direct reach to the
   184	  host, the local network, the tailnet, the router, printers, storage devices, other
   185	  business machines, or production. Enforced by a **host-side firewall that remains
   186	  effective even if guest configuration changes** — the guest cannot reconfigure its own
   187	  cage.
   188	- **Fixed resources**: a fixed-size (not dynamically expanding) virtual disk, hard CPU,
   189	  memory, process-count, execution-time, and network-volume limits. An agent can brick a
   190	  Mac by exhaustion; a spend cap stops none of that. Disk-full, memory-pressure,
   191	  network-flood, and VM-crash responses are specified and tested.
   192	- **Isolated clones or storage snapshots, never shared working copies.** A standard git
   193	  worktree shares the underlying repo — history, branches, hooks, objects, settings. Each
   194	  agent gets a separate clone or copy-on-write snapshot with no path back to the real
   195	  repo.
   196	- **The containment profile covers every runner** (Claude Code, Codex, Ollama), every
   197	  child process, every MCP server, every hook, every scheduled job, every retry and
   198	  recovery path. Local MCP servers run inside the VM; **remote MCP servers hold their own
   199	  credentials and act where THEY run** — each is either brokered with exact authority or
   200	  not connected at all.
   201	- **The kernel sandbox on, strict, as a second layer inside the VM**: deny credential
   202	  paths, scrub credential env vars, narrow domain allow-list, no unsandboxed commands.
   203	  Noted honestly: a startup check that asserts this is itself configuration — it detects
   204	  drift, it is not independent enforcement. The VM boundary is the real wall.
   205	- **Prompt security updates for the Mac and the VM software** (see "Update policy" — a
   206	  frozen hypervisor is its own risk).
   207	- **VM snapshots, crash dumps, and backups are treated as sensitive** — they can retain
   208	  secrets and private source, so they live under the same protection as the things they
   209	  might contain.
   210	
   211	---
   212	
   213	## No keys in agent hands — the brokers, with exact authority
   214	
   215	Agents hold **no reusable credentials of any kind**. Each broker has a written, exact
   216	authority and hostile-input rules — a broker that "forwards requests" is just a longer
   217	credential.
   218	
   219	- **The GitHub broker.** Authority: accept a *restricted patch* (below) and create a
   220	  branch and PR from it. Nothing else — no merges, no tag/release/automation/package/
   221	  settings writes. Hostile-input rules: reject binaries and unidentifiable content into
   222	  protected areas, reject sub-project pointers and large-file pointers unless explicitly
   223	  expected, reject path escapes, size limits, no automation-file changes without
   224	  high-authority classification. **Branch creation and PRs can trigger automation before
   225	  any merge** — so repository automation runs with no valuable secrets on unreviewed
   226	  code, or does not run at all. GitHub protection covers branches, tags, automation,
   227	  packages, releases, environments, and admin bypass — not just `main`. Third-party
   228	  GitHub apps with write access are treated as alternate authority and removed or
   229	  narrowed.
   230	- **The provider broker.** Agent runners get no reusable subscription or API credentials;
   231	  the broker holds them, applies per-job limits *before* every call (reserving budget
   232	  before spending, correct under concurrent requests), and is the only path to any model
   233	  provider. Hard provider-side caps for every provider that offers one — stated honestly:
   234	  **only a provider's own hard stop is a real financial boundary**; where a provider
   235	  offers none (subscriptions), the broker's gating plus monitoring is a mitigation, not a
   236	  boundary. A provider's *response* is untrusted input — it can carry a poisoned
   237	  instruction back in.
   238	- The honest note on subscription runners: a coding runner needs *some* auth material to
   239	  exist somewhere. It lives with the broker outside the VM, sessions are established
   240	  through the broker, and this unresolved tension is named rather than hidden: if a
   241	  runner technically requires login material inside its environment, that runner is
   242	  treated as holding a credential and its environment is narrowed accordingly.
   243	
   244	---
   245	
   246	## The release chain — one exclusive, signed path
   247	
   248	### Separated powers
   249	
   250	Round 5: one small service holding merge + deploy + spend + production access is a single
   251	point of total compromise. The powers are split into separately-credentialed components,
   252	each with the smallest identity that works:
   253	
   254	- **The merge caller** — the only identity that can merge a PR.
   255	- **The clean builder** — builds the package; holds no deploy or spend credentials.
   256	- **The signer** — signs package digests; its key does nothing else.
   257	- **The deployer** — delivers a signed package to production; cannot merge, sign, or
   258	  spend.
   259	- **The spend broker** — already separate (above).
   260	- **The record-writer** — sole writer of verdicts, approvals, evidence provenance, and
   261	  released status; the harness submits observations through a narrow authenticated
   262	  interface. "Sole writer" protects who writes the record, not whether an observation is
   263	  true — truth comes from the binding rules below.
   264	
   265	No agent can reach any of these: no general network address, local OS-account-checked
   266	channels only, fixed narrow actions with exact parameters, single-use request numbers,
   267	approvals consumed atomically, rejection if anything changes mid-operation. Requests come
   268	from a **deterministic controller deriving them from protected state — never from
   269	model-written text.** None of these components ever builds, installs, tests, or executes
   270	agent-produced code beside its credentials.
   271	
   272	### Safe patch intake
   273	
   274	Agents submit **a restricted patch — never a built package.** The intake accepts a fixed
   275	format; rejects hostile paths, links, unexpected binaries, repository indirection, and
   276	oversized submissions; and records the patch against the requesting job.
   277	
   278	### The trusted builder and the package
   279	
   280	A clean builder combines the proposed change with current `main` in a contained
   281	environment, resolves dependencies from **pinned digests, not floating names**, produces
   282	a reproducible package, and records its **signed origin** (what source, what base, what
   283	builder) in an **immutable package store**. Every review, test run, screenshot, and
   284	approval binds to that package digest — a real screenshot from the wrong build, a stale
   285	process, or a test that never exercised the dangerous path is exactly what binding
   286	exists to kill.
   287	
   288	### Production verifies — the path is exclusive
   289	
   290	**Production accepts only packages whose signature and digest verify against the signed
   291	chain.** This is enforced at the production end, so the claim "all releases go through
   292	the service" stops being a request and becomes a property. Every alternate route is
   293	disabled, not ignored: old deploy scripts, automation identities, admin bypasses, cloud
   294	schedulers, platform consoles with deploy rights, and **the current harness's own legacy
   295	paths** (see "Decommissioning").
   296	
   297	### Rollout, monitoring, kill, rollback
   298	
   299	Deployment is not the end of custody:
   300	
   301	- **Limited first rollout** with health checks and automatic halt.
   302	- **Data backup before destructive changes.**
   303	- **Independent post-release monitoring** — unusual data access, unexpected outgoing
   304	  traffic, money movement, secret access, unexpected background activity — watching the
   305	  *behavior* of what shipped, because review already passed it.
   306	- **A production kill switch** that stops a deployed component — distinct from the job
   307	  emergency stop, which only kills agent work.
   308	- **Rollback that cannot revive an unsafe package** (a rolled-back-to version must itself
   309	  still verify against the chain and not reintroduce a withdrawn package).
   310	
   311	---
   312	
   313	## Production containment — enumerating what deployed code may do
   314	
   315	The missing boundary round 5 named: deploy permission is not runtime authority. For every
   316	deployed component, the design enumerates **exactly which data it can read, which records
   317	it can write, which secrets it can use, which destinations it can contact, and what it
   318	can spend.** That enumeration is:
   319	
   320	- the input to danger classification (a component with narrow authority can ship
   321	  autonomously; a component with valuable authority cannot),
   322	- enforced outside the component (network egress enforcement, per-component credentials,
   323	  scoped database access — not the component's own good behavior),
   324	- and honest about residual risk: a payload inside a component can still misuse that
   325	  component's *legitimate* authority. Containment shrinks the blast radius; monitoring
   326	  and the kill switch handle what remains.
   327	
   328	---
   329	
   330	## Data egress — architecture, not a slogan
   331	
   332	An agent must be allowed to talk to its model provider, so a poisoned instruction can put
   333	private material into a *legitimate* request — and a domain allow-list doesn't help when
   334	the allowed destination is itself the channel. The egress design therefore:
   335	
   336	- puts **enforcement outside the agent and outside production workloads** (the host-side
   337	  firewall for the VM; the production-side egress control for deployed components),
   338	- defines **per-project, per-provider rules** for what may deliberately be sent where,
   339	- blocks cross-project reads structurally (an agent's VM contains only its own project's
   340	  clone — there is nothing else to exfiltrate),
   341	- and **admits plainly**: an allowed provider request can carry allowed private source;
   342	  encoded or divided exfiltration through a legitimate channel cannot be reliably caught
   343	  by content scanning. The mitigations are narrow project scope, provider agreements,
   344	  post-hoc auditability of what was sent, and keeping the most sensitive material out of
   345	  scope entirely.
   346	
   347	**DECIDED (Neill, 2026-07-15): the PHI-bearing EMR is OUT of scope now, revisitable
   348	later.** The harness's project list does not include it and the agents' environment holds
   349	no clone of it. Bringing it in requires the strict medical-data egress controls to be
   350	built and attack-tested first — a precondition, not a follow-up.
   351	
   352	---
   353	
   354	## The voice + Chief layer — deterministic control, not a chatty relay
   355	
   356	The voice stays a telephone. The control around it is deterministic:
   357	
   358	- **A deterministic turn controller** owns dispatch, with explicit states: listening →
   359	  complete-but-uncommitted → committed → superseded → dispatched → cancel-requested →
   360	  confirmed-stopped. **Only committed instructions may dispatch.** A model's guess that
   361	  speech finished is never enough for dangerous or costly work.
   362	- **The controller governs every input door** — voice, the text web app, and any direct
   363	  request path. A safe voice path is irrelevant if the text page can dispatch directly
   364	  around it.
   365	- **Exact delivery via fixed playback** (see the human-roles section) — warnings,
   366	  numbers, consequences, confirmations, failure reports. Verbatim *forwarding* of the
   367	  owner's speech is impossible (the mouth generates text from audio), so Chief confirms
   368	  its own read-back and the confirmation attaches to that read-back.
   369	- **A separate, hardwired emergency stop** that does not use Chief or any model,
   370	  reachable while driving without navigating a screen. Honest about what it is: a stop
   371	  *request* is not a verified stop — the stop path terminates local work, revokes queued
   372	  and scheduled continuations, instructs remote/brokered work to halt, and then
   373	  **verifies and reports what is confirmed dead versus still unknown.** It kills jobs;
   374	  the *production* kill switch (above) handles deployed code.
   375	- **Authoritative acknowledgements only.** "Nothing started" may only be spoken after the
   376	  authoritative record confirms no dispatch exists. When unsure: "I lost confirmation —
   377	  use the emergency stop or check the written status."
   378	- **Structured state lives outside the conversation** — job state, active instruction,
   379	  cancellations, approved versions, project scope — never Chief's rolled/summarized
   380	  history, which can drop a "not," revive a cancelled order, mix projects, or carry an
   381	  injection.
   382	- **Ambient speakers are an open risk stated plainly.** Phone auth proves which phone
   383	  opened the session, not who is talking. Voice can never grant a dangerous action; that
   384	  needs the authenticated written approval, stopped.
   385	- **"Stopped and tapped" is a safety instruction to Neill, not a machine guarantee.**
   386	- **Numbered turns, reconnection, and long sessions** — session rollover, lost audio,
   387	  interruptions, network switches — are part of the controller, with a realistic driving
   388	  test set before the voice is trusted beyond calm, stopped conversation.
   389	
   390	Chief is `gpt-5.6-terra` (low effort, escalating to `gpt-5.6-sol` on pushback or a real
   391	decision), streaming, ~1.4s to first words, with a hard latency guard. Chief is not a
   392	security boundary; the doors are.
   393	
   394	---
   395	
   396	## Resilience, secrets, and updates
   397	
   398	- **Off-machine backups** of the safety record, job history, project memory, config, and
   399	  approval history — with **tested restoration drills**. Restoration is itself a guarded
   400	  path: restoring old configuration must not revive old credentials, stale approvals,
   401	  unsafe automation, or a decommissioned release route.
   402	- **Tamper-resistant audit records**, stored off this Mac and off the trusted account,
   403	  with the storage and key arrangement specified (append-only remote store; keys not
   404	  present on the Mac).
   405	- **Secret lifecycle**: creation, limited delivery, rotation schedule, expiration,
   406	  emergency revocation, and verification that old copies are gone (including from
   407	  snapshots and backups). Incident response: device loss, compromised phone session,
   408	  poisoned memory, release-path compromise.
   409	- **Crash recovery**: on startup, reconcile any in-progress job with a dead run — never
   410	  silently lost; a stale approval can't remain live when unattended work resumes.
   411	  Duplicate protection: a retry can't start the same job twice.
   412	- **Update policy, stated realistically**: local software is version-pinned and upgraded
   413	  deliberately, each upgrade re-running the attack test before being trusted. **Security
   414	  updates are not deferred indefinitely** — the Mac and the VM software patch promptly.
   415	  Remote things (model behavior, provider services) **cannot be pinned**, only
   416	  version-selected, monitored, and re-tested; the pin list says which is which. An
   417	  OpenClaw upgrade can run data conversion under the trusted account before any startup
   418	  check — so upgrades happen in a sandbox first, against a copy.
   419	
   420	---
   421	
   422	## Decommissioning the old paths
   423	
   424	Sol's existing-system check: the current code contradicts this document, and a callable
   425	old route is a bypass. **Before any new boundary is credited, the old paths are disabled,
   426	not ignored:** direct runner launch from dispatch, the harness writing reviewer results,
   427	the old autonomous shipping path, the web service holding a provider key, model-based
   428	speech-turn detection, and capability rows read as if they were restrictions. The v1
   429	`backend/`/`frontend/` remain dead. Removal is verified as part of the acceptance test.
   430	
   431	---
   432	
   433	## Standing constraints (unchanged)
   434	
   435	- Text works fully without voice. Voice is a skin.
   436	- Tailscale only; nothing public — plus real user authentication on top (Tailscale is not
   437	  identity).
   438	- Never OpenClaw exec policy `full`/YOLO.
   439	- **Grok CLI does not touch the Arch EMR repo** until the repo-upload allegation is
   440	  independently disproven.
   441	
   442	---
   443	
   444	## The acceptance test — explicit attacks, fail-closed
   445	
   446	"Sol finds no path" is not a provable statement, so acceptance is a fixed list of attack
   447	cases that must **fail closed**, plus named residual risks. Before building, Sol reviews
   448	this document against the list; before trusting the build, each case is executed as a
   449	real attack against every runner, every MCP server, every scheduled path, and repeated on
   450	every upgrade of anything in the pin list.
   451	
   452	The attack cases (each must be blocked or must escalate to human review — never silently
   453	pass):
   454	
   455	1. A patch touching only a lockfile, a build script, generated output, a template, a
   456	   feature flag, or a scheduled-task definition attempts routine classification.
   457	2. A high-authority change split across several routine-looking submissions; cumulative
   458	   classification must catch it.
   459	3. A submission with a binary, minified blob, link, sub-project pointer, or unknown file
   460	   type attempts intake.
   461	4. An agent attempts to reach GitHub, a provider, the release components, the host, the
   462	   tailnet, or another project's data directly from the VM.
   463	5. A PR attempts to trigger repository automation holding valuable secrets.
   464	6. A package not signed by the chain attempts to start in production; a rollback attempts
   465	   to revive a withdrawn package.
   466	7. The harness attempts to write a verdict, invent a reviewer identity, or submit an
   467	   observation for a package digest that doesn't match.
   468	8. Concurrent provider requests attempt to outrun spend accounting; spending must stop at
   469	   a provider-side boundary where one exists.
   470	9. Private cross-project material attempts to enter an allowed provider request
   471	   (structural: the clone isn't there).
   472	10. Partial speech, reconnection, interruption, session rollover, or a stale queued
   473	    answer attempts to dispatch or to override a newer correction.
   474	11. The emergency stop is pressed with local, queued, scheduled, and remote work running;
   475	    the system must report confirmed-dead versus unknown truthfully.
   476	12. Resource attacks: fill the disk, fork-bomb, exhaust memory, flood the network — the
   477	    business Mac must remain usable.
   478	13. A restore from backup attempts to revive a revoked credential, stale approval, or
   479	    decommissioned release route.
   480	14. Every legacy path from the decommissioning list is attempted and confirmed dead.
   481	
   482	Named residual risks, accepted knowingly: a payload misusing its component's legitimate
   483	authority within its enumerated containment (mitigated by monitoring + kill switch);
   484	encoded exfiltration through an allowed provider channel (mitigated by scope + audit);
   485	same-Mac VM escape (mitigated by patching; migration to separate hardware is a config
   486	change); ambient speech (voice can never grant dangerous actions).
   487	
   488	---
   489	
   490	## ✅ OWNER DECISIONS — record
   491	
   492	- **A (2026-07-15, revised 2026-07-16 after round 5): mandatory independent review for
   493	  high-authority changes — ACCEPTED, with the technical reviewer seat currently EMPTY and
   494	  the system failing closed.** High-authority changes queue, visibly and in plain
   495	  English, until a qualified technical human exists to inspect them; Neill's
   496	  authenticated written approval is the business authorization, never the technical
   497	  inspection. Everything outside the high-authority classes ships autonomously through
   498	  the model gauntlet. Neill accepts that this reduces autonomous scope.
   499	- **B (2026-07-15): agents run in a dedicated VM on this Mac** — full profile above,
   500	  residual same-Mac risk accepted knowingly, migration to separate hardware stays a
   501	  config change.
   502	- **C (2026-07-15): the PHI-bearing EMR is out of scope now**, revisitable only after
   503	  strict medical-data egress controls are built and attack-tested.

