# Verdict: NOT APPROVED

I would not approve this design to be built as the architecture of record.

Four failures are decisive:

1. An agent can create or alter the written description of a component’s authority and thereby make it appear harmless. The document fails closed only when that description is absent—not when it is unreviewed or false.
2. It never says a queued high-authority change is forbidden from being merged into the shared source. Once merged, it can contaminate later “routine” packages.
3. The separated powers are separate identities, but not independent decision-makers. A compromised builder, signer, record-writer, or central controller can still create something the rest of the chain accepts.
4. Production verifies a package signature, but not the complete release decision or complete running system. Runtime settings, commands, secrets, scheduled work, external feature flags, and old-but-signed packages remain alternate ways to change production.

## 1. The 12 required changes

“Genuinely addressed” means the document names an actual boundary sufficiently clearly to build and test. “Addressed only in words” means it states the desired result without defining the enforcement that produces it.

| # | Requirement | Verdict | Load-bearing sentence and result |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Dedicated VM or separate host | **Genuinely addressed at design level** | The [VM profile](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:176) requires no sharing, no local-network access, broker-only outgoing traffic, fixed resources, and host-side enforcement. That is a buildable containment design. The same-Mac escape risk is honestly accepted. |
| 2 | Release facts written outside the harness | **Addressed only in words** | “The record-writer [is the] sole writer” and the harness submits “authenticated observations” ([release powers](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:260)). This controls who writes rows, not who can manufacture facts. There is no signed evidence from each reviewer, tester, builder, or approver that the record-writer cannot forge. |
| 3 | Qualified independent human review | **Addressed only in words** | The distinction between technical inspection and Neill’s business approval is now correct, and high-authority work is said to remain pending ([human roles](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:90)). But “qualified,” “independent,” who appoints the reviewer, how the reviewer authenticates, and how approval is protected from forgery are undefined. |
| 4 | Isolated clones, never shared working copies | **Genuinely addressed** | Every agent receives a separate clone or isolated snapshot “with no path back to the real repo” ([storage boundary](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:192)). That delivers the required design, subject to build testing. |
| 5 | No direct GitHub write power | **Addressed only in words** | The GitHub broker is limited to a “restricted patch,” branch, and pull request ([broker section](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:219)). But the patch format is unnamed, and the document does not define how branch creation, repository settings, existing automation, filters, links, or unusual paths are safely handled. |
| 6 | Exact package-based release chain | **Addressed only in words** | Patch-only intake, a clean builder, pinned digests, signed origin, immutable storage, and production verification are all stated ([package chain](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:274)). But no independent process proves the builder’s package matches the reviewed source. A compromised builder can insert code and a compromised signer can bless it. |
| 7 | Broker all provider access | **Addressed only in words** | The broker reserves budget before each request and is declared the only provider path ([provider broker](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:230)). The document explicitly leaves subscription-runner credentials unresolved. It also lacks a complete inventory proving voice, reviewers, remote tools, retries, and scheduled work all use it. |
| 8 | Enforced data-egress policy | **Addressed only in words** | External enforcement points and the allowed-provider limitation are correctly identified ([egress design](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:332)). But the actual per-project rules and production destination rules do not exist. “Post-hoc auditability” also does not prevent disclosure. |
| 9 | Full VM containment profile | **Genuinely addressed at design level** | The profile covers every runner, child process, tool server, hook, schedule, retry, and recovery path, with fixed resources and external firewall enforcement. The design is specific enough to implement and attack-test. |
| 10 | Deterministic voice controller | **Addressed only in words** | The states and all-input-door rule are specified ([turn controller](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:358)). The event that turns speech from “complete but uncommitted” into “committed” is still undefined. So is who may create that event. |
| 11 | Backups, audit, secret handling, incident response | **Addressed only in words** | Off-machine backup, append-only audit, restoration checks, and secret rotation are listed ([resilience section](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:396)). The actual independent account, key owners, deletion protection, retention, restore authority, and recovery root of trust are not designed. |
| 12 | Honest core claim and explicit acceptance tests | **Addressed only in words** | The claim is now accurately limited and 14 attacks are listed. But several attacks against the new design are absent, including false authority descriptions, compromised builders and signers, queued-change contamination, external activation, and a compromised central controller. |

Bottom line: three are genuinely addressed at design level. Nine remain requirements rather than working designs.

## The 14 previously missing designs

| # | Previously missing design | Current verdict |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Qualified-reviewer mechanics | **Still missing.** The role is declared, but qualification, appointment, independence, authentication, removal, conflicts, review environment, and signed approval are absent. |
| 2 | Production authority per application | **Still missing.** The document says an enumeration exists for every component, but supplies none. No reader can determine what any actual component may read, change, reveal, contact, or spend. |
| 3 | Authenticated owner approval | **Genuinely addressed for stolen-session protection.** A device-bound, short-lived, single-use approval tied to the package and action is the right mechanism. It still fails if the web page itself is compromised and shows Neill one action while asking his device to approve another. |
| 4 | Fail-closed classification | **Addressed only in words, with a direct defect.** An absent authority description fails closed; an unreviewed or dishonest description does not. |
| 5 | Safe patch intake | **Addressed only in words.** “Fixed format” is stated, but the format, parser, allowed operations, naming rules, and treatment of renames and repository filters are not specified. |
| 6 | Trusted builder | **Addressed only in words.** There is no exact isolation profile for the builder, no complete toolchain record, and no independent reproduction proving the output matches source. |
| 7 | Production-side verification | **Genuinely addressed for rejecting unsigned packages.** It does not establish that every other part of the running release—settings, commands, secrets, schedules, side programs—was approved. |
| 8 | Rollout and rollback | **Addressed only in words.** Limited rollout, health checks, and withdrawal are bullets, not a state machine with identities and enforcement. |
| 9 | Post-release detection | **Addressed only in words.** No monitor location, trusted data source, response threshold, alert recipient, retention, or protection from the workload is defined. |
| 10 | Production kill switch | **Addressed only in words.** “Stops a deployed component” does not say how it defeats a hostile component, revokes stolen credentials, cancels persistence, or protects the kill mechanism itself. |
| 11 | Web and direct-request authentication | **Addressed only in words.** The document says the person is authenticated but provides no user roles, session rules, direct-request credentials, or authorization table. |
| 12 | Separation of dangerous powers | **Addressed only in words.** Separate account names are listed, but one central controller appears able to request all actions. Services on one Mac under one administrator are not independent fault boundaries. |
| 13 | Secret lifecycle | **Addressed only in words.** The lifecycle is enumerated, but no secret store, delivery method, rotation owner, backup treatment, or proof-of-deletion mechanism exists. |
| 14 | Realistic update policy | **Genuinely addressed at policy level.** Local pinning, deliberate upgrades, prompt security patches, sandboxed upgrades, and the inability to pin remote behavior are correctly distinguished. Build proof is still required. |

## 2. The classifier: walking the evasion list

| Evasion | What the new rules do | Does it still get through? |
|---|---|---|
| Build and installation behavior | Explicitly sends build and packaging changes to human review. | **Direct changes are caught.** An ordinary helper, data file, repository filter, or external tool used by the build can still escape because the document has no complete build-input graph. |
| Lockfile-only change | Explicitly high-authority. | **Caught**, assuming every lockfile and equivalent dependency record is recognized. |
| Transitive dependency change | Explicitly included; the builder also uses pinned digests. | **Mostly caught.** Base images, build tools, system packages, compiler plug-ins, and other toolchain inputs are not explicitly included. |
| Generated or minified code | Explicitly high-authority. | **Caught.** |
| Generation inputs | Templates are explicitly covered. | **Incomplete.** Schemas, data files, localization files, model files, and other “known harmless” inputs that generate behavior are not comprehensively covered. |
| Runtime-interpreted configuration | Routing, prompts, flags, schedules, templates, policies, and database statements are listed. | **Repository changes are caught. External settings are not.** A production console, remote feature-flag service, cloud scheduler, database setting, or administrative API can activate behavior without a patch. |
| Ordinary source with valuable authority | Any change to a component holding valuable production power is high-authority. | **Caught only if the component map and authority description are true.** They are presently untrusted records. |
| Repository indirection | Links, sub-project pointers, and large-file pointers are blocked or escalated. | **The named forms are caught.** Case collisions, unusual names, renames, repository filters, build-time downloads, and content fetched from another repository remain unspecified. |
| Dormant loader | Cumulative review should connect the loader and later activating change. | **Caught only within a correctly mapped component and baseline.** Activation through existing production data or an external flag still bypasses release classification. |
| Split authority | Each component with private-data access, important writes, secrets, money, or broad outgoing access should be high-authority. | **Cross-component combinations still get through.** There is no map of information flowing through shared queues, logs, caches, files, or records. Two individually “narrow” components can combine their powers. |
| Cumulative drift | Everything since the last qualified human review is considered. | **The principle is correct but the baseline is undefined.** Renames, component moves, shared libraries, parallel branches, restores, rollbacks, and already-merged queued work can break the calculation. |
| External activation | Repository feature-flag and schedule definitions are high-authority. | **Still gets through when activation occurs outside the repository.** |
| Classifier changes | Always high-authority. | **Caught**, but changes to the component map, authority descriptions, enforcement policies, and “harmless” file rules are not explicitly given the same protection. |

The largest classifier hole is this sentence:

> “The enumeration of component authority is reviewed by the technical reviewer when the seat is filled, and until then components without an enumeration are treated as high-authority.”

That means an enumeration merely needs to exist. It does not need to have been independently reviewed. An agent can write “this component has no sensitive access,” assign code to it, and attempt autonomous release.

The safe rule must be:

> No component is autonomously releasable until its authority description and the external controls enforcing it have both been independently reviewed. Any change to the description, component boundary, enforcement, or shared dependency is itself high-authority.

## 3. The empty reviewer seat and its queue

There is no explicit emergency release exception, and the document explicitly says Neill cannot substitute for the technical reviewer. Those two points are clear.

There are nevertheless several routes around the queue:

1. **Unreviewed authority descriptions.** The direct bypass above.
2. **Changing component boundaries.** Moving code under a supposedly harmless component is not explicitly high-authority.
3. **Merging before approval.** The document never states that a pending high-authority change cannot enter the shared source branch. The builder later combines other work with that branch.
4. **Forging the technical reviewer’s record.** Device-bound authentication is defined for Neill, not for the technical reviewer. “Approval is recorded against the digest” is not proof of who produced it.
5. **Trusted-system updates.** Urgent operating-system, OpenClaw, broker, classifier, signer, and monitoring updates will create pressure to bypass an indefinitely empty seat. No rule resolves this conflict.
6. **Restore and recovery.** An old backup may contain an earlier reviewer assignment, approval, authority description, or classifier state. The document says this must not happen but provides no mechanism.

The queue itself is attackable:

- An agent can flood it with thousands of dangerous submissions and bury the important one.
- Pending items can become stale when the shared source changes.
- No rule says a pending item is immutable; edits could be mistaken for the reviewed item.
- No priority, quota, cancellation, retention, or ownership rules exist.
- Pull requests can run repository automation while waiting.
- A hostile patch can attack the reviewer’s viewer or build environment.
- Repeated near-identical requests can exhaust or fatigue the eventual reviewer.
- There is no safe process for appointing, replacing, suspending, or revoking a reviewer.
- “Naming one later fills the seat” does not say who is allowed to name one.

There is also a bootstrapping contradiction. The classifier, signer, record-writer, production verifier, approval service, and containment policies are themselves high-authority. With the reviewer seat empty, the first trustworthy installation of this system cannot be approved. The document needs to say plainly that work may be prepared in isolation, but the new security system cannot become trusted or control production until a qualified reviewer has inspected the initial implementation.

## 4. Separated powers and the exclusive path

Separate credentials reduce the damage from some failures. They do not yet prevent one compromised component from producing a valid-looking release.

| Compromised component | What it can still do under this design |
|---|---|
| Merge caller | Merge arbitrary code into the shared source and contaminate every later build. The document does not require it to verify technical approval before merging. |
| Builder | Produce a malicious package that does not match the reviewed source. “Reproducible” does not help unless a separate trusted builder actually reproduces it and compares the result. |
| Signer | Sign an unapproved or withdrawn package. Since production’s main test is the signature, the signer can create what production treats as valid. |
| Deployer | Select an old, wrong-environment, or otherwise inappropriate signed package. It may also be able to change startup commands, environment settings, attached secrets, network rules, or side programs; none of those are covered by package verification. |
| Spend broker | Spend up to each provider’s real external cap, disclose source to providers, and withhold or alter responses. Where the provider offers no hard cap, the broker compromise reaches the full account. |
| Record-writer | Append false reviews, approvals, evidence claims, or release status. An append-only store stops erasure; it does not stop the authorized writer from appending lies. |
| Harness or central controller | Submit observations, create jobs, consume provider spending, and potentially request merge, signing, and deployment sequentially. The controller’s identity and limits are not separated from the trusted harness. |
| Web UI | Flood routine work, consume spending, misstate critical text, or show Neill one action while obtaining a device signature for another. A normal passkey proves user presence, not that the trusted device displayed the actual transaction. |

Production-side signature verification is necessary, but not sufficient to make the path exclusive. A signature proves only that a particular key signed a particular digest.

Production must also verify a signed release authorization containing:

- the exact package;
- the exact source and independently verified build result;
- the target environment;
- the complete startup command and runtime settings;
- attached secrets, permissions, network policy, side programs, and schedules;
- the required human and model approvals;
- an expiry and one-time release number;
- current withdrawal and revocation status;
- the permitted rollout stage.

The production enforcement layer must reject unsigned runtime changes and must not be alterable by the deployer. Otherwise the deployer can run signed code under unreviewed settings.

The current design also lacks signer-key rotation, compromise recovery, trust-root replacement, package withdrawal distribution, and a controlled administrator emergency path. An old signed package will continue verifying unless production checks a protected, current withdrawal record.

## 5. Claims still resting on good behavior, configuration, or records

These remain ineffective as security boundaries:

- **“Qualified reviewer.”** No qualification or identity mechanism exists.
- **“Authority enumeration.”** It is currently a written claim, not a measured result derived from external controls.
- **“Sole writer.”** It does not establish the truth of what is written.
- **“Deterministic controller.”** Deterministic processing of poisoned or forged state still produces the wrong action reliably.
- **“Separated identities.”** Separate accounts on one administrator-controlled Mac are not independent security domains.
- **“Clean builder.”** Clean at startup does not prove the output corresponds to source.
- **“Reproducible package.”** Nobody is required to reproduce it independently.
- **“Immutable store.”** The account, deletion protection, administrator access, and key ownership are not specified.
- **“Independent monitoring.”** No independence mechanism or trustworthy data source is named.
- **“Production kill switch.”** There is no enforcement mechanism and no response for stolen credentials or persistence created before the kill.
- **“Rollback cannot revive a withdrawn package.”** No monotonic withdrawal record or production check is defined.
- **“Fixed critical text.”** A compromised web service or harness can change stored text unless the templates and transaction details are protected independently.
- **“Data backup before destructive changes.”** A record saying “backup succeeded” is not proof that it is complete and restorable.
- **“No agent can reach release services.”** This rests on local account configuration and an undefined controller interface.
- **“Exact broker authority.”** The document describes intended authority but provides no complete broker inventory or enforceable request grammar.
- **“Every alternate route is disabled.”** The transition and administrator emergency route are undefined.

## 6. The seams, again

| Seam | Remaining bypass |
|---|---|
| Runners | Subscription credentials remain unresolved. Self-updates, plug-ins, login helpers, remote execution, and provider sessions are not fully designed. |
| Local and remote tool servers | Local servers are placed in the VM, but remote servers act outside it with their own credentials. No actual server-by-server authority list exists. |
| Hooks and build steps | A builder may execute hostile installation or generation behavior before human approval. Its exact network access, disposable environment, and store access are not defined. |
| Schedules | Repository, cloud, platform, database, and external feature-flag schedules can activate behavior without a new package. |
| Retries | Single-use request numbers are stated, but there is no complete treatment of a crash after the external action succeeds but before it is recorded. A retry could merge, sign, spend, or deploy twice. |
| Recovery | Startup reconciliation covers jobs, not every partial merge, signing operation, deployment, withdrawal, secret rotation, or kill operation. |
| Voice | The commit action remains undefined. Ambient speech cannot approve a dangerous release, but it can still create work, flood queues, and consume money. |
| Text and direct web requests | The document says one controller governs them, but user roles and direct-request authorization are missing. The present web service has no real person-level authentication. |
| Brokers | Each broker remains a concentrated authority over its own domain. A compromised provider broker can spend; a compromised GitHub broker can create hostile branches and automation triggers. |
| GitHub | Administrator access, existing repository owners, third-party applications, fork automation, webhook replay, and settings changes remain alternate authority. |
| OpenClaw | Sandbox-first upgrades help, but the upgrade’s authorization, signed origin, plug-ins, state conversion, rollback, and initial trust are unspecified. |
| Other updates | Builder tools, base images, operating-system packages, tool servers, and deployment-platform changes are not all included in classification. |
| Backups and restore | No trusted restore controller, signed backup manifest, protected withdrawal list, or fresh credential issuance is defined. A restore can reconstruct the bypasses the document says are dead. |
| Production settings | Startup commands, secrets, access rules, routes, external flags, scheduled functions, database procedures, and side programs can alter behavior without changing the signed package. |
| Legacy system | The document correctly says all old routes must die, but it does not define the safe cutover order. During the rebuild, old and new authority can coexist. |

The live system confirms the transition problem. It still directly launches runners, lets the general harness record verdicts, permits old autonomous “shipping,” holds a provider key in the web service, and uses model-based speech completion: [direct launch](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:122), [general verdict writer](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/db/jobs.py:257), [old shipping path](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/dispatch.py:173), [provider key](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/server.py:298), [model-based speech completion](/Users/user/code-projects/chief-command/harness/server.py:337).

The document acknowledges these routes, but it does not state that old release and production authority will be frozen before work on the replacement begins.

## 7. What is missing entirely

The document still lacks complete designs for:

1. **The actual authority inventory.** A table for every real deployed component listing exact data, records, secrets, destinations, outputs, spending, and administrative actions.
2. **The authority source of truth.** Authority must be derived from enforced credentials and policies, not an agent-written description.
3. **Cross-component authority.** Information flows through queues, logs, caches, databases, files, and shared services must be assessed as a whole.
4. **Reviewer governance.** Qualification, appointment, identity, hardware-backed approval, independence, conflicts, replacement, revocation, and safe review environment.
5. **The blocked-review queue.** Immutability, quotas, priority, cancellation, stale-item handling, replay protection, and an absolute ban on merging pending dangerous work.
6. **The initial bootstrap.** Who independently reviews and authorizes the first classifier, signer, verifier, record-writer, and containment controls.
7. **The complete release state machine.** The exact order of classification, build, review, approval, merge, rebuild or verification, signing, rollout, and final release.
8. **Independent build verification.** A second controlled reproduction or equivalent proof that the package matches the reviewed source and pinned toolchain.
9. **Signing-key governance.** Hardware protection, allowed signing policy, rotation, revocation, compromise response, and trust-root replacement.
10. **A complete signed release description.** Package plus environment, runtime settings, attached permissions, secrets, schedules, side programs, and rollout stage.
11. **Administrator and emergency authority.** Someone ultimately controls the platform. That access cannot simply be declared absent; it needs strict custody, multiple-person use, logging, and recovery rules.
12. **Compromise tolerance for the trusted services.** The threat model focuses on agents but the design claims separation will survive one trusted component being compromised. It does not specify which single failures must be tolerated.
13. **Monitoring and kill architecture.** Trusted sensors outside the workload, alert thresholds, independent operators, network isolation, credential revocation, persistence removal, evidence capture, and recovery.
14. **Control of production changes that are not packages.** External flags, database procedures, cloud settings, identities, network rules, secrets, schedules, and deployment commands.
15. **A safe transition plan.** The old release system must be disabled before it can build, approve, or deploy the new security system.
16. **Availability protection.** Queue flooding, broker flooding, record-store exhaustion, denial of the kill switch, and denial of reviewer access are not addressed.

## 8. Approval decision and required changes

No, I would not approve this design to be built.

### Must change the design

Ranked in order:

1. **Close the authority-description bypass.** An authority description is invalid until independently reviewed and matched to real external enforcement. Changes to the description, component boundary, shared dependencies, permissions, or enforcement are always high-authority.

2. **Design the reviewer and queue completely.** Give the reviewer hardware-backed identity, define qualification and appointment, make every queued item immutable and digest-bound, prevent queue flooding, and prohibit pending high-authority changes from entering the shared source.

3. **Resolve the empty-seat bootstrap.** State that the initial security system cannot become trusted or control production until a qualified reviewer has inspected it. Define how urgent security patches are handled without creating an owner or emergency bypass.

4. **Write the release state machine.** Specify the exact order from patch intake to production. No step may rely on a later step to repair an earlier unsafe action. In particular, clarify when merging occurs and how a moving shared source invalidates approvals.

5. **Make separation survive one trusted-component compromise.** The central controller must not be able to command every power by itself. Each service must independently verify signed prerequisites. A compromised record-writer must not be able to forge source-signed reviewer, builder, owner, or monitoring evidence.

6. **Constrain the builder and signer.** Independently reproduce the package. Prevent the signer from signing arbitrary digests. Production must require both a package signature and a separate, current release authorization.

7. **Cover the whole running release.** Sign and verify the package, environment, command, runtime configuration, permissions, secrets, side programs, schedules, network policy, rollout stage, and withdrawal status. Block dynamic loading of unsigned behavior.

8. **Provide the actual production authority tables and controls.** Enumerate every component now, including shared services and cross-component information flows. “To be enumerated during implementation” is not sufficient.

9. **Design key, withdrawal, administrator, and emergency control.** Include compromise recovery, trust-root rotation, old-package withdrawal, and tightly controlled platform-administrator access.

10. **Turn monitoring, kill, and rollback into mechanisms.** The kill path must work against a hostile workload, isolate networking, revoke credentials, cancel persistence, preserve evidence, and report damage it cannot undo.

11. **Protect owner and reviewer transactions from a compromised web page.** The trusted device must display or independently verify the actual package and action being approved. A server-chosen passkey challenge is insufficient by itself.

12. **Expand the acceptance test.** Add explicit attacks for false authority descriptions, component moves, pending-change contamination, malicious builders, compromised signers, compromised deployers, forged records, a compromised controller, UI transaction swapping, external activation, cross-component authority, and unsigned runtime configuration.

13. **Define the safe cutover.** Freeze and remove the old release powers before the replacement is allowed to produce trusted records or production packages.

### Must be proven during the build

After those design changes, the build must prove:

- Every runner, tool server, hook, schedule, child process, retry, and recovery route stays inside its assigned boundary.
- No reusable credential is readable inside an agent environment.
- The VM cannot reach the Mac, local network, tailnet, production, or release services directly.
- Resource exhaustion inside the VM cannot make the business Mac unusable.
- An unreviewed authority description or component move cannot produce routine classification.
- Pending high-authority work cannot enter another package through the shared source.
- The independently rebuilt package exactly matches what production starts.
- A compromised builder, signer, deployer, record-writer, harness, controller, or web UI cannot individually create an unauthorized production release.
- Production rejects unsigned settings, commands, side programs, schedules, and withdrawn packages—not only unsigned main packages.
- GitHub branches and pull requests cannot run automation holding valuable authority.
- Spending stops at real external caps under concurrent and retried requests.
- Technical-review and owner approvals cannot be forged, replayed, swapped, or applied after the source changes.
- External flags and schedules cannot activate unapproved behavior.
- Monitoring still detects behavior when the deployed workload lies or suppresses its own logs.
- The production kill path works while the workload is actively resisting it.
- Restoring a backup cannot revive credentials, approvals, identities, packages, automation, or release paths that were withdrawn.
- Every legacy direct-launch, direct-write, direct-provider, and old-release route is demonstrably dead before cutover.

The rewritten document corrects the round-5 conclusions, but it does not yet turn them into an architecture that survives the attacks it now acknowledges.
